Skyulf 0.9.2 Bundle: **{{if eq .training_layout "multi_target"}}independent target models{{else}}{{.task}}{{end}}** with **{{.engine}}** training and **{{.inference_mode}}** inference, using **{{.compute_mode}}**
compute. Training and preprocessing fit in one bounded Python process on this compute. {{if eq .inference_mode "spark"}}Scoring runs a partition-safe MLflow pyfunc on Spark workers and keeps keyed predictions distributed.{{else}}Scoring uses a bounded frame in the saved pandas/Polars engine.{{end}}

## Inference mode and capacity

The setup choice is `inference_mode={{.inference_mode}}`. Here `local` means
whole-frame scoring, including on Databricks compute. The separate SDK
`runtime="local"` value selects integration behavior and does not detect machine
location; UC Delta publication requires `runtime="databricks"`. `engine` chooses
pandas or Polars. Estimate the rows and
bytes in **each scoring run** and the memory available to the selected compute.
There is no universal row threshold for Spark. Local inference uses the configured
`max_rows` and `max_input_mb` bounds. Spark inference distributes scoring through
`mlflow.pyfunc.spark_udf`; those bounds still apply to pandas training and do not
cap the total scoring population. Keep training samples and budgets sized for the
driver even when scoring runs on many workers.

{{if eq .inference_mode "spark"}}Spark inference requires `engine=pandas` and a fitted artifact whose effective
preprocessing chain is partition safe. Unsupported steps, row-changing behavior,
custom scoring callbacks, temporal history and unsupported model-set composition
fail before publishing predictions. The error identifies the unsupported step;
revise the recipe and retrain, or initialize a local-inference project. Existing
fitted Polars artifacts are not converted automatically.

See [FEATURE_TABLES.md](FEATURE_TABLES.md) for the current certified estimator,
preprocessing and declarative composition scope. Model menus also cover local
inference; Bundle validation does not certify a fitted artifact for Spark.

`config/inference.yml` records `spark_udf_env_manager={{if eq .compute_mode "serverless"}}local{{else}}virtualenv{{end}}` and
`spark_udf_prediction_batch_rows=10000`. The worker environment must contain
the exact model and Skyulf dependencies recorded with the registered artifact.
Certified packages embed an exact-source Skyulf wheel and its source hash;
{{if eq .compute_mode "serverless"}}serverless `local` uses the declared task environment, including the same
Skyulf wheel and dependency versions on the driver and workers. Verify those
versions and source hash on the selected compute before deployment.{{else}}the job cluster uses `virtualenv` to install that wheel and the recorded
dependency pins in an isolated worker environment. Verify worker startup on the
selected compute before deployment.{{end}} The batch
setting bounds each model prediction call (1â€“100000 rows), not the total input
or incoming Arrow allocation. Databricks manages Arrow transport; serverless
does not permit changing its Arrow batch configuration. An environment failure
stops scoring without changing the selected manager.
{{end}}

## Preprocessing diagnostics

Set `defaults.preprocessing_probe: true` in `config/training.yml` to inspect
saved preprocessing after fitting. It defaults to `false`. Each fitting run uses
the saved/reloaded artifact and at most the first 256 holdout rows, with an 8 MiB
input/output limit, and logs `preprocessing_probe.json` to MLflow.

Open the fitting run's **Artifacts** tab or **Preprocessing diagnostics** in the
training report. A step requiring group/window/global context can report
`requires_context`, with later steps `not_run`. This does not refit, measure model
quality or change promotion policy. Read [PREPROCESSING_CONTEXT.md](PREPROCESSING_CONTEXT.md)
for a runnable example, all statuses and explicit lag/rolling continuation.

## Configure the project

New projects use these YAML files directly. No migration command or Python
model declaration file is needed.

| File | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `config/features.yml` | Optional upstream feature groups and merged observations; empty `groups` disables the feature job |
| `config/training.yml` | Sources, inputs, target, split/CV, models, tuning, weights and training quality policy |
| `config/inference.yml` | Scoring source/output, model selection, change policy and whole-set publication |
| `config/pre_split.yml` | Ordered fixed eligibility filters before splitting |
| `config/preprocessing.yml` | Ordered built-in and custom steps fitted inside each training fold |
| `src/features/pre_split.py` | Custom row-filter functions selected by pre-split YAML |
| `src/features/preprocessing.py` | Custom transformation functions selected by preprocessing YAML |
| `src/features/scoring.py` | Per-model and combined business output functions |
| `src/features/custom/` | Optional scoring callbacks and advanced class examples |
| `src/features/groups/` | Optional Spark functions producing shared Delta feature tables; configured in `config/features.yml` |
| `deployment/targets.yml`, `deployment/variables.yml` | Workspace, UC bindings, identities, schedules and compute |

`training.yml` has `version: 1`, `defaults` and named `models`. A model entry
inherits shared defaults and overrides complete values. Nested mappings are
replaced, not deep-merged. For example:

```yaml
version: 1
defaults:
  training_layout: multi_target
  task: regression
  model:
    type: ridge_regression
    params: {alpha: 1.0}
models:
  revenue:
    target_column: revenue
  cost:
    target_column: cost
    model:
      type: linear_regression
```

This is an excerpt; retain the generated source, registry, keys and resource
limits. Quote string identifiers such as `"on"` or `"null"`. YAML booleans are
`true`/`false`, and `null` clears a nullable setting. Duplicate keys, unknown
fields and simultaneous YAML/Python owners are errors. Omitted optional settings
use validated runtime defaults; inactive CV/date/sampling settings are omitted.
Do not add another `workflow.json` or Python model-definition file.

{{if eq .training_layout "single_model"}}Edit `models.main.model` for estimator type and fixed parameters; edit the sibling
`tuning` for search settings. Exactly one named model is required.
{{else if eq .training_layout "model_competition"}}Each `models` entry is one candidate. Candidates share the target, split, CV and
quality policy in `defaults`; override the model, tuning, preprocessing recipe,
decision threshold or explainability per candidate. Shared CV ranks candidates,
then only its winner receives the independent holdout and champion checks. A
failed winner does not automatically promote the runner-up. Bound total search
with `defaults.competition_max_trials` and `competition_max_candidates`.
{{else}}Each `models` entry is one independent target. Its target, inputs, model,
recipes, split/CV and quality policy can differ. Keep every target out of all
model inputs. Training packages the complete set as one release; its activation
and publication settings live under `inference.yml` ? `model_set`. Component
aliases are not the set's release mechanism.
{{end}}

Model syntax is compact and maps to the existing saved Core configuration:

```yaml
models:
  main:
    model:
      type: random_forest_regressor
      params: {n_jobs: 1}
    tuning:
      strategy: random
      metric: rmse
      n_trials: 10
      random_state: 42
      search_space:
        n_estimators: [100, 200]
        max_depth: [5, 10]
```

Remove `tuning` to fit the configured model directly. Generated search spaces are
editable snapshots of Core's model catalog; later catalog changes do not silently
replace them. An empty `search_space: {}` asks the installed Core version for its
current automatic space. Update search axes when changing model or ensemble
members. Fixed parameters must not conflict with searched axes.

Grid searches admit at most `max_candidates` (default 1000, maximum 10000).
Random, halving-random and Optuna use `n_trials` (maximum 1000). Halving and Optuna
controls live in `tuning.strategy_params`; Optuna's timeout stops between fits.
Ensemble members, voting weights, stacking final estimator/CV and calibration
live in `model.params`. Stacking's internal CV differs from evaluation CV.

`decision_threshold`, `explainability`, `weight_column`, `preprocessing_recipe`
and `pre_split_recipe` live in `defaults` or a model entry. Competition shares
one weight column and pre-split policy. The optional top-level `pipeline` may
contain empty `preprocessing`/`modeling` structure for tooling; actual model
settings belong to named models, and feature steps belong to their phase YAML files.

### Custom Python and saved replay

See [Preprocessing and custom features](PREPROCESSING.md) for the six-step
customer example, the exact YAML/Python placement and optional prediction writes.

Edit the ordered lists in `config/pre_split.yml` and `config/preprocessing.yml`.
Keep custom functions in `src/features/pre_split.py` and `preprocessing.py`; select
them with a YAML `custom` path and `params`. Adapt the example column names. `column_step` creates columns, `fitted_step` learns
state on training rows and `filter_step` applies fixed eligibility. Relative
imports keep custom functions available after model reload. Do not perform I/O
in imports or custom factories. Each phase has one owner: do not export a Python
builder for a phase configured in YAML. Existing Python-only projects still work.

Training freezes `src/features/`, excluding the root `groups/` directory, as a
versioned 64 KiB package snapshot. Spark producers in `groups/` execute separately
in the feature job. Keep custom model functions in their phase modules; do not
import producers from model recipes. See [FEATURE_TABLES.md](FEATURE_TABLES.md) for the stage boundary.
Declare
bounded data files in `src/features/assets.json` as `files: [...]` (the file itself
uses JSON), and read them with `read_project_asset` from
`skyulf.inference.project_package`. Paths stay inside the package; symlinks are
rejected. Code, captured recipe declarations, assets and exact dependency pins share the
snapshot budget.
Use `distribution==version` in `src/features/requirements.txt` and install those
versions in training/scoring environments; loading verifies them without installing.

YAML and Python edits apply to the next training load. Saved models retain resolved
settings, recipe declarations, fitted preprocessing and frozen source; scoring and lifecycle replay do
not reread editable YAML or Python. Changing rules/assets/pins requires a new model.

```powershell
python src/tools/smoke.py
python src/tools/preview.py --action train
```

Smoke validates YAML, model configuration and source syntax without executing
custom builders. Preview executes trusted builders, but does not read cloud data
or train. Review selected columns, source names and positive `max_rows` and
`max_input_mb` limits. An omitted `training_version` pins the latest Delta snapshot
at initialization; an integer pins that version. Random full-snapshot training
needs no dates. Temporal training needs its event column and window.

Only notebooks referenced by the selected jobs are generated. Adding omitted
SHAP/chart/recovery capabilities later needs the corresponding notebook,
dependencies and graph as well as its setting. Initialize into a separate directory
and copy reviewed resources while preserving edited YAML and Python functions.

### Class and row weights

Set classifier class weights in `model.params`, for example
`params: {class_weight: balanced}`. `null` disables them. Keep a class weight fixed
by removing its search axis; to tune it, omit the fixed parameter and retain the
axis under `tuning.search_space`. Supported classifiers may also use row weights.

Set `weight_column: importance` in `defaults` or a target entry. Omit it or use
`null` for unweighted fitting. Competition uses one shared column. Every active
fold requires finite nonnegative values with positive total weight. Weights are
not normalized. Declared weight columns remain reserved across branches and cannot
be features, targets, keys, group columns or timestamps.

Weights affect estimator fitting; selection, holdout and threshold metrics remain
unweighted. Built-in row changes carry explicit row positions; unsupported custom
row mapping fails. Synthetic sampling needs an explicit supported weight policy.
Source snapshots preserve the training column and saved evidence records its
aggregate statistics and ordered digest. Logs do not print raw weight vectors.
Scoring uses the saved model and needs no weight column. Weight-only edits do not
count as fresh labels for automatic retraining; train explicitly after such edits.

{{if eq .training_layout "multi_target"}}
### Select published results

The initializer asks `model_set_name`, `model_set_output_mode` and `model_set_table_name` for this
layout. Blank names use project defaults; explicit names use exactly that object
name with the target's catalog/schema and deployment suffix added automatically.
`model_set_name` names the registered model set, not a table (default:
`<project_name>_set`). `model_set_table_name` names its physical result table
(default: `<project_name>_set_scores`). View defaults are
`<project_name>_predictions_<branch>` and `<project_name>_business_results`.
Edit `publication` in `config/inference.yml` ? `model_set` to change the selection:

| Mode | Physical table | Consumer views |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `all` (default) | Model predictions, model outputs and combined business results | None |
| `combined_only` | Combined business results, keys, rule status and set provenance | None |
| `separate_views` | All results saved once | One per selected model, plus combined results when rules exist |

All modes still run each model's saved scoring rules before combined rules.
`combined_only` requires active combined rules in the saved set; configure,
train and approve them first. It omits intermediate predictions from storage.
Switching between this mode and the full schema requires a new table name.

For `separate_views`, setup also asks `model_view_prefix` and
`combined_view_name`. omitted `model_views` creates a view for every saved branch;
a mapping selects only its listed branches and gives each a custom name:

```yaml
model_views:
  revenue: "{catalog}.{output_schema}.revenue_predictions{resource_suffix}"
  cost: "{catalog}.{output_schema}.cost_predictions{resource_suffix}"
combined_view: "{catalog}.{output_schema}.profit_results{resource_suffix}"
```

`model_views: {}` exposes no individual model views. Views contain record keys,
the relevant output/status columns and set provenance; model columns retain their
branch prefix. Views are projections, not additional prediction tables. All read
one committed backing table. A rule error fails the job before any new data is
written; earlier successful results remain. View setup happens before the data
write and can leave empty/old-result views if setup fails. Existing unrelated
objects are never replaced. Changed view sources/projections require new names;
removing a view from configuration does not delete an existing catalog object.

For example, after scoring `id=101`, inserting `id=102` lets the next batch append
its prediction. Updating a feature on `id=101` or deleting that row is different:
the default `source_change_policy="reject"` fails the batch. Choose
`rebuild_on_change` in Bundle setup (or `config/inference.yml` ? `model_set`) to rebuild the
complete current snapshot with the selected set when UPDATE/DELETE is observed.
Deleted rows disappear, corrected rows and business results are recomputed,
and lag/rolling history starts from the corrected snapshot. Ordinary inserts
still append. The full snapshot must fit `max_rows` and `max_input_mb`; otherwise
the job fails and keeps previous results. Permission failures still fail.
Expired CDF history fails unless `auto_rebuild_on_cdf_expiry=true` enables
recovery of recognized history loss; this is a separate scoring policy.

The two questions have independent purposes: `model_change_mode` controls a
new model-set selection; `source_change_policy` controls changed/deleted source
rows. A source-triggered rebuild uses the selected set for ALL rows, even if
earlier rows were scored with older versions in append mode. There is no
retraining. Old projects without the new setting retain `reject`. This option
currently belongs to model-set scoring; the standalone single-model insert
scorer keeps its existing source-change rejection contract.

### Set quality and activation

Bundle setup asks `model_set_promotion_policy` (default `manual_approval`). Edit
`promotion_policy` in `config/inference.yml` ? `model_set` to choose `automatic` later.
The set policy is separate from individual branch policies, which stay manual.
Set each branch's `metric`, `quality_threshold`, optional `quality_gates` and
`min_improvement` in its named model entry in `config/training.yml` ? `models`.
For example, RMSE with threshold 10 means RMSE <= 10; F1 with threshold 0.8
means F1 >= 0.8. Use limits suitable for your task, including ensemble branches.

The first set needs passing absolute thresholds for every branch. Replacements
require no branch to regress on its selected metric against the corresponding
component in the pinned champion set. At least one branch must improve by its
`min_improvement` (with a positive gain even when the minimum is zero). Equal
peers are allowed; an all-tied set stays challenger. For example, lower revenue
RMSE with unchanged risk accuracy can promote the complete set. All absolute
gates still apply, including additional metrics configured in `quality_gates`.
The quality policy and baseline are saved with the set and evaluated on saved holdout rows.
Editing training.yml cannot change a previously registered set's policy.
Branch names and registered component names must match the existing set layout.

Automatic mode checks all gates after packaging. A failed gate leaves the current
champion unchanged and reports the failed components. A complete passing set
becomes champion. Missing evidence, changed baselines and execution errors fail
the job. Scoring stays a separate job. Manual approval checks the same gates.

For manual approval, use the `train` job's `lifecycle_action=approve`, the concrete set
`candidate_version`, and `expected_champion_version` (`none` for first activation).
Approval executes the saved complete set on a bounded scoring-source snapshot and
requires each component and rule to produce at least one result, then verifies
every saved component quality policy before changing the set alias.
Old sets without quality pins remain scoreable but need retraining/packaging for
approval through this Bundle flow.
Save the returned promotion receipt. To restore its prior complete set, use
`lifecycle_action=rollback`, `promotion_receipt_json`, and the expected current
champion version.

Every completed training candidate is nominated as the set's `challenger`. A new
candidate moves the displaced contender to `previous_challenger`. Approval removes
the promoted version's challenger alias and preserves the old champion as
`previous_champion`. The individual component aliases remain unchanged.

Use `lifecycle_action=reject` with the current set `candidate_version`,
`expected_champion_version` and `rejection_reason` to record a manual rejection.
The rejected set stays visible as challenger until a new candidate replaces it;
its `approval_status=rejected` prevents later approval. Rejection does not run
training or scoring, or require passing quality gates. An identical repeat reuses
the original receipt. A changed reason, candidate or champion fails explicitly.
Rollback restores the complete prior set and preserves unrelated challenger history.

The same `score` job calls `score_models.py` for this layout. It pins the set
champion once (or uses `score_model_version`) and publishes the full keyed result
with set identity. Scoring loads saved code, so changing editable rules requires
training/packaging and approving a new set. Both jobs remain serialized with
`max_concurrent_runs=1`; they must exclusively own set aliases and output writes.
Append retains earlier rows with their original set version; full rebuild
atomically replaces compatible output on a set change. Temporal carry changes
require full rebuild. Declare rule dependencies as exact pins in
`src/features/requirements.txt`; conflicting component pins are rejected.
The remaining lifecycle instructions below apply to `training_layout=single_model`.

{{end}}
### Optional decision thresholds

Classifier recipes accept `decision_threshold` with `off` (default), `manual`
or `auto`. Automatic mode reserves `validation_fraction` of the training
partition before fitting preprocessing or selecting model parameters. With
`validation_fraction: 0.25`, the saved model fits 75% and thresholds use the
other 25%; there is no full-training refit afterward. Off/manual modes do not
reserve these calibration rows. Final holdout rows remain independent.

Threshold objectives and reported metrics are unweighted even when estimator
fitting uses sample weights. `f1_weighted` averages classes by support; it does
not apply row weights or optimize weighted business costs. The saved
`decision_threshold.json` records fit/calibration counts and selection scores.

Outputs use `probability_0`, `probability_1`, etc., in saved `manifest.classes`
order. These classes retain original target labels after target encoding.
See the [decision-threshold guide](https://flyingriverhorse.github.io/Skyulf/user_guide/databricks_bundle/#optional-classification-decision-thresholds)
for policy examples and multiclass semantics.

### Optional evaluation charts

Bundle initialization asks `evaluation_charts_enabled` (default: `false`). Choosing
`true` adds a separate `generate_charts` task after model evaluation, plus
`matplotlib==3.10.0` in the training environment. Charts do not retrain models.

| Layout | Images and destination |
| --- | --- |
| Single | Saved model diagnostics in a `Charts` child of its training run |
| Competition | CV leaderboard and selected model diagnostics share a `Charts` child of the parent training run; final holdout is never used to rank candidates |
| Model set | Each component has its own `Charts` child; a separate `Charts` child of the set parent contains the overview |

Regression includes actual versus predicted, residuals and error distribution.
Classification includes raw/normalized confusion matrices and per-class metrics;
ROC/PR and calibration require model probabilities. Tree models expose native
feature importance; linear models expose signed coefficients when available.
Saved CV folds, tuning trials and training loss are plotted only when present.
Full-holdout metric summaries use existing scalar values and separate metric scales.

Open the **Charts** tab using the links printed by `generate_charts`. Images
are indexed in dedicated `Charts` child runs with no training parameters, so
MLflow's image-grid header does not repeat the long parameter list. The original
training run retains all parameters, metrics and model artifacts. Its
`skyulf.charts.run_id` tag links to the latest successful chart run.

MLflow can automatically create image cards from logged image keys. If a card
is missing from your saved layout, choose **Add chart -> Image grid** and select
a key such as `single_actual_vs_predicted` or `competition_leaderboard`.
The task output is plain text: created image keys grouped under their MLflow
Charts links. Sample counts, skipped diagnostics and `charts_run_id` remain in
`evaluation_charts/report.json` on the parent training run. Component reports
keep the source `run_id` and the separate chart `destination_run_id`.

`config/training.yml` ? `defaults` controls the saved sample and display budgets:

```yaml
evaluation_charts:
  enabled: true
  max_rows: 2000
  max_features: 20
  max_classes: 20
```

Enabling charts saves a deterministic bounded sample of actual heldout predictions,
probabilities and labels in the evaluated model's MLflow artifacts, subject to
the experiment's access and retention policies. Raw features and record keys are
excluded. Sampling happens after full-holdout evaluation, preserving temporal
feature context. Maximums are
10,000 rows, 50 displayed features and 50 classes; each sample has a 16 MiB budget.
Pairwise coefficient outputs are capped at 20 boundaries. Sample diagnostics are
labelled with their row count; full-holdout scalar metrics are never overwritten.
Missing classes or unavailable model capabilities are reported explicitly.

The task is an independent leaf with no retries. A chart failure can mark the
overall job failed but does not undo model decisions or repeat training. Inspect
the chart task and `skyulf.charts.status`; do not repair an old lifecycle invocation.
Setting `enabled` to `false` disables an already deployed chart task. Adding charts
to a project generated without them also requires adding the generated task and
Matplotlib dependency, then redeploying; the flag alone cannot change a job graph.

### Optional SHAP explanations

Bundle setup asks whether to enable SHAP (default: off). Enabling it adds pinned
`shap==0.49.1` and `matplotlib==3.10.0` dependencies to the training job, on both
serverless and policy clusters. Scoring does not compute explanations.

The setup budgets apply to single models, each competition candidate and all
generated multi-target branches. Edit `explainability` under `defaults` in
`config/training.yml`, or in a named model entry for an independent override:

```yaml
explainability:
  method: shap
  max_samples: 100
  max_features: 30
  max_display_samples: 3
```

Remove the field or set it to `null` to disable computation.
For an existing project, also add the dependencies above to the training job's
environment/libraries before enabling SHAP manually. Projects generated with
SHAP disabled have no report tasks: regenerate with SHAP enabled, or add a
`shap_report.py` task depending only on its matching training task and passing
that task's `reference_json`. Validate and redeploy the updated graph.

`max_samples` (1-200) bounds training rows used for computation. `max_features`
(1-50) guards the transformed model input width: exceeding it returns unavailable;
it does **not** select the top features or drop columns. `max_display_samples`
(0-50, at most `max_samples`) controls saved sample waterfalls. Zero retains only
global importance. These settings apply per model, so an eight-model run can do
eight times the explanation work.

Each enabled `shap_<name>` task displays a mean-absolute importance chart and expandable
waterfalls with transformed feature values. Red contributions increase the
explained output; blue contributions decrease it. Base plus contributions is in
the explainer's output units, which may be raw scores/log-odds for classifiers,
not probabilities. Importance describes model behavior, not causal effects.
Sample numbers identify positions in the report, not business record keys.

MLflow saves `explanations.json` and a portable `explanations.html` with embedded
PNG charts, the exact run link and fitted model URI. No JavaScript/CDN is needed.
Competition children retain their own reports; the winner's report is also
retained on the parent with its child-run provenance. Unavailable explanations
show their reason; disabled training does not import SHAP or plotting packages.
Inputs are bounded training rows through already fitted inference preprocessing;
holdout rows and target/time metadata are not explanation features.

### Training graph and notebook files

Each `train_<name>` performs a real fit and retains its split, preprocessing,
model, CV and tuning information. Its `shap_<name>` task is a separate leaf:
it displays that exact run's saved report and has no outgoing dependencies.
SHAP computation still happens during the fit; the leaf does not retrain the
model. Unavailable explanations show their saved reason. A report-task failure
is visible in the job but does not prevent model selection or set assembly.

Only SHAP tasks display HTML charts and reports. All other notebook tasks print
their structured JSON result, including training, comparison, model decisions
and scoring. Task values and downstream job handoffs use the same saved results.

- Competition: `initialize_run -> choose_action -> load_data -> prepare_dataset`,
  then one training task per candidate. `select_best_model` waits for every
  training task, adopts the fitted winner without retraining, and continues to
  `register_model -> evaluate_model -> model_decision -> training_report`.
  Loser runs and their reports remain in MLflow; losers are not registered.
- Multi-model: `initialize_run -> choose_action`, then one training task per
  target. `register_model_set` waits for every model, packages the complete set
  and nominates its challenger. `evaluate_model_set` checks each component's
  quality and comparison with the pinned champion. `model_decision` applies
  the set's promotion policy or an explicit approve/reject/rollback action.
  Champion/challenger aliases belong to the set; component versions stay pinned.
  `training_report` closes the invocation even when a training task fails.
  After a successful report, `scoring_requested` starts `run_batch_scoring` only
  when the enabled handoff has a verified champion transition.
- Single model: the existing `train_and_tune` task retains training information;
  `validate_model` verifies the fitted candidate before `register_model`.
  `shap_model` displays its separate report when SHAP is enabled.

The graph uses meaningful model names; notebook implementation is shared:

| File in `src/jobs` | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `initialize_run.py` | Pin single/competition source, recipes and champion |
| `initialize_models.py` | Pin every multi-model recipe, source version and set baseline |
| `load_data.py`, `prepare_dataset.py` | Load and split the shared single/competition dataset |
| `train_and_tune.py` | Fit a single model |
| `train_model.py` | Fit the named competition candidate or multi-model branch |
| `shap_report.py` | Display one training task's saved SHAP report |
| `select_best_model.py` | Select the complete competition's winner from training-side CV |
| `validate_model.py` | Verify the single fitted candidate before registration |
| `register_model.py`, `evaluate_model.py`, `model_decision.py` | Existing single-model registration and alias controls |
| `register_model_set.py` | Package the complete set and nominate its challenger |
| `evaluate_model_set.py` | Evaluate every component against its quality policy and pinned champion |
| `model_set_decision.py` | Apply set promotion policy or an explicit approve/reject/rollback action |
| `training_report.py`, `models_report.py` | Final single/competition or multi-model status |
| `score.py`, `score_models.py` | Saved single-model or model-set scoring |

Changing parameters or a recipe keeps the graph unchanged. After adding,
removing or renaming entries in `config/training.yml` ? `models`, run:

```bash
python src/tools/refresh_training_graph.py
databricks bundle validate --strict --target {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}}
databricks bundle deploy --target {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}}
```

The refresh script preserves schedules, compute and operator settings. A changed
model list with an old deployed graph is rejected before any model is trained.
Tasks can run concurrently when compute permits; each task's row/byte/search
limits still apply separately. Existing serialized job admission, frozen
receipts and no-repair/no-retry rules remain in effect.

### Training parameters in MLflow

After training, open the model's MLflow experiment run and select **Parameters**.
Competition candidates and multi-target branches each record their own settings:

| Parameter | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `tuning_strategy`, `tuning_model_type` | Search strategy and selected model family |
| `tuning_requested_metric`, `tuning_metric` | Requested objective and resolved scorer |
| `tuning_requested_trials`, `tuning_trials` | Configured trial budget and actual Core trial count |
| `tuning_random_state`, `tuning_timeout`, `tuning_n_jobs` | Search seed, timeout and workers |
| `tuning_strategy_params`, `tuning_search_space` | Strategy controls and effective search space |
| `tuning_best_params.<name>` | Each parameter selected by the final search |

Large parameter previews point to the complete `tuning.json` artifact. Existing
`cv_*` parameters remain alongside these fields; `tuning_best_score` is a metric.
With nested CV, Parameters shows the final search on all training rows; individual
outer-fold selections remain in `tuning.json`. These fields appear on new training
runs after updating the Core wheel; existing experiment runs are not backfilled.

Fixed and tuned models also expose their fitted configuration in Parameters:

| Parameter | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `model_type`, `model_params.<name>` | Model family and actual constructor settings, including defaults and tuned values |
| `ensemble.base_models`, `ensemble.strategy` | Ordered member names and voting/stacking strategy |
| `ensemble.voting`, `ensemble.weights` | Applicable voting type and weights in member order |
| `ensemble.final_estimator`, `ensemble.cv` | Stacking meta-model and its internal folds, separate from workflow CV |
| `split_strategy`, `split_test_size`, `split_random_state`, `split_stratify` | Active random holdout controls, separate from CV and tuning seeds |
| `split_start`, `split_holdout_start`, `split_cutoff`, `split_event_column` | Temporal holdout boundaries and clock column; inactive random controls are omitted |
| `preprocessing_recipe`, `pre_split_recipe` | Selected recipe or declared builder default; `inline` means no project recipe metadata |
| `preprocessing_steps`, `pre_split_steps` | Ordered step names for each phase |

`training_parameters.json` retains complete summaries when a preview exceeds the
display limit. Non-JSON constructor objects are described by type; their nested
constructor settings are separate entries, and the fitted artifact retains the
actual objects. `builder_default` means the builder has no named recipe default;
`none` means that optional builder is absent. Previously saved artifacts without
recipe metadata still work.

The top-level `cv_*` fields are the only fold settings. They score search
candidates; `cv_enabled: false` uses an internal training-only validation split. There is
no second post-search CV report. The search objective (`rmse`, `accuracy`, etc.)
is separate from the final holdout comparison metric (`heldout_rmse`,
`heldout_accuracy`, etc.). `search_random_state`, `cv_random_state`, and the
top-level split `random_state` control different randomness. Core's `nested_cv`
option repeats tuning independently inside each outer training fold. `cv_folds`
sets outer folds; optional `cv_inner_folds` sets inner folds. A separate final
search selects the saved model. Each search gets the configured trial/time
budget. Outer evaluation and final-search scores are reported separately.
The final holdout is reserved for model comparison.

`cv_nested_type` chooses `auto`, `k_fold`, `stratified_k_fold`,
`time_series_split`, `group_k_fold` or `stratified_group_k_fold` at both nested
levels. Temporal CV uses `event_column` and `cv_shuffle: false`; `cv_gap`,
`cv_test_size` and `cv_max_train_size` are row counts (null maximum expands the
training window). Nested temporal CV requires a temporal final holdout. Missing
times and timestamps tied across fold boundaries fail explicitly.

Group CV uses `cv_group_column` only as split metadata. Keep it out of
`input_columns`. Random final holdout splits whole groups; set `stratify: false`.
A temporal final holdout must also have disjoint groups. Missing groups fail.

For binary nested search, set `"tune_threshold": True` in the model definition to select
thresholds from inner out-of-fold probabilities. Outer and final holdout labels
remain untouched. The saved artifact applies the independent final threshold;
`tuning.json` and the readable results retain threshold evidence. Models without
probabilities and multiclass threshold tuning fail explicitly.

Preprocessing starts empty; ensemble and tuning settings are generated per model.
Keep `pipeline.preprocessing` and `pre_split_steps` empty in
JSON. Put custom pairs in the features package and enable them in the appropriate
builder. Approval and scoring use saved code, even after project source changes.
See [setup and advanced inputs][setup] and [custom recipes][recipes].

Install the matching Skyulf wheel in your local environment, then from this project:

```powershell
python src/tools/preview.py --action train
python src/tools/preview.py --list-models
python src/tools/preview.py --list-preprocessors
```

Preview executes your trusted recipe builders and checks configuration; it does
not read source data or train. Keep data access/training out of imports and
builders. For another target, pass `--catalog`, `--input-schema`, `--output-schema`,
`--metadata-schema` and `--resource-suffix` using that target's bindings.

## 2. Build and deploy

Set `deployment/artifact.json` once: `source` is the path to a Skyulf Core source
checkout or a release wheel, and `version` is the required Core release. Relative
paths resolve from this project; keep the source outside generated `dist/skyulf/`.
Bundle deployment prepares the wheel automatically,
checks its package name and version, and records its SHA-256 in
`dist/skyulf/build.json`. A content digest in the wheel's build tag gives changed
bytes a distinct deployment/cache identity while preserving the release version
required by existing saved models. The receipt matches the uploaded wheel bytes.
To prepare it before strict validation:

```powershell
uv run --no-project python src/tools/build_wheel.py
```

From this generated project, with your authenticated CLI profile:

```powershell
databricks bundle validate --strict -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile <profile>
databricks bundle deploy -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile <profile>
```

Both jobs install `deployment/requirements.txt`. Initial Optuna and optional
model/ensemble choices populate their exact package pins automatically. Update
this file when changing those choices in Python later. Custom preprocessing and
scoring pins in `src/features/requirements.txt` are included in both jobs.
`deployment/train-requirements.txt` adds only the requested SHAP/chart packages.
These are direct dependency pins, not a lock of every transitive dependency;
validate a changed environment before deploying it over an existing model.

Targets are paired by environment: `test/test_development`,
`syst/syst_development` and `prod/prod_development`. Personal targets are optional,
use user-scoped names and start with paused schedules. Configure the workspace
and separate shared/development schemas in
`deployment/targets.yml` and `deployment/variables.yml`; see
[deployment setup](deployment/README.md) for external CI/CD, identity choices and
optional personal targets inside each environment. The source tables
must already exist; enable Delta Change Data Feed on the scoring source before
new rows arrive. The first score processes all existing rows in that source.
Record keys must be unique, nonnull STRING/BIGINT columns.

{{if eq .compute_mode "policy_cluster"}}
Review the approved cluster policy, runtime, node type and cost tag for each
workspace. See the checkout's `skyulf-core/templates/databricks/examples/policy-init.example.json`.
{{else}}
The jobs use serverless Python environment 4. Policy clusters are a separate
initialization choice.
{{end}}

Deployment creates three jobs and uploads code/configuration; input tables are
not created. Training and scoring clocks are independent. The separate
monitoring schedule starts paused until references are prepared and verified.
See [deployment and schedules][schedules] and [existing-project migration][migration].

## 3. Train, inspect and approve

```powershell
databricks bundle run -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile <profile> train
databricks bundle run -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile <profile> score
```

Run `score` after a model is selected. When handoff is enabled, first inspect its
child score run; a separate score invocation is only needed for new rows or recovery.

The `train` job has one active run, with queued training and operator actions:

| Task | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `initialize_run` | Validate settings and pin source version, recipe and expected champion |
| `choose_action` | Select training or an explicit approve/reject/rollback action |
| `load_data` | Read the bounded pinned source and save its verified dataset |
| `prepare_dataset` | Apply fixed cleanup and eligibility rules, then save training/holdout partitions |
| `train_and_tune` | Fit preprocessing inside training folds, run configured CV/tuning, and save the fitted pipeline |
| `validate_model` | Verify the single fitted candidate |
| `select_best_model` | Show the competition leaderboard and selected winner |
| `register_model` | Check heldout evaluation before registering and nominating the candidate |
| `evaluate_model` | Compare the registered candidate with the pinned champion on the same holdout |
| `model_decision` | Apply promotion policy or execute an explicit approve/reject/rollback action |
| `training_report` | Finalize the run and publish verified results and next actions |
| `register_monitor` | Enroll the activated model, competition winner or model-set components before scoring |
| `monitor_model` | Measure the successful scoring commit and save shared monitoring results |
| `monitoring_report` | Display separate drift, observed performance and performance loss evidence |
| `evaluate_retraining` | Show both trigger reasons and apply one shared guarded training request |
| `scoring_requested` | Check whether scoring was requested |
| `run_batch_scoring` | Invoke the existing score job |

The separate score job has the `score` task. With CDF expiry recovery enabled,
`recovery_needed` routes recognized expiry to `recover_predictions`, and
`scoring_report` shows the actual scoring or recovery result. Open **`training_report`** for
metrics, the decision and ready-to-copy `next_actions`. Search details are in
`train_and_tune`; champion comparison is in `evaluate_model`. Score results are
in the child or independent score run. Manual actions skip data and training
and use `model_decision` directly. Finalization can run after failure; that
does not imply a successful result.

`prepare_dataset` applies only fixed transformations and filters. Learned
preprocessing and feature engineering stay inside `train_and_tune` so each CV
fold learns from its own training rows. With tuning disabled, that task fits
the configured model directly. With `training_layout=model_competition`, each
named candidate is fitted and `select_best_model` verifies the complete shared-CV
leaderboard before the winner reaches registration. Other candidates remain
inspectable MLflow runs without registered versions.

Bounded source and split datasets are saved as Parquet artifacts under the
MLflow run's `lifecycle/data/`. Downstream training verifies their saved digests
and preserves split membership without rereading the source. These artifacts
contain training data: apply the experiment's access and retention policy.
Registration and approval retain their source/evidence replay checks.

This project's policies are:

- `score_model_selection={{.score_model_selection}}`: pinned version or champion.
- `promotion_policy={{.promotion_policy}}`: manual approval or automatic gates.
- `score_handoff={{.score_handoff}}`: disabled or score after an alias change.
- `auto_rebuild_on_cdf_expiry={{.auto_rebuild_on_cdf_expiry}}`: optional full rescore
  after recognized expiry of retained Delta change history.

Set a numeric `quality_threshold` before the first champion approval/promotion.
Later promotions also need positive improvement meeting `min_improvement`.
Training a candidate never changes a saved scoring pin. Only the serialized train
job identity should write lifecycle aliases; enforce exclusive ownership externally.

Use the train job's **Run now parameters** for operator actions:

| `lifecycle_action` | Required inputs |
| --- | --- |
| `train` | Clear operator evidence fields |
| `approve` | Copy `next_actions.approve`: candidate and expected champion |
| `reject` | Copy `next_actions.reject` and add `rejection_reason` |
| `rollback` | Copy `next_actions.rollback`, including `promotion_receipt_json` |

Use explicit `none` for a first champion. Its initialization has no prior champion
to roll back to. A later completed promotion returns rollback inputs. Evidence
is revalidated; editing a registry alias directly is not approval.
See [results and next actions][results] and [operator walkthrough][operators].

## 4. Score new rows and recover

The independent score job accepts `score_model_version` for a one-run concrete
version override; blank uses the configured selector. Handoff uses the configured
selector. `incremental_append` keeps old predictions and scores new inserts;
`full_rebuild` builds a versioned generation and switches the view after success.
An unchanged replay is a no-op. [Prediction behavior and model changes][predictions].

`auto_rebuild_on_cdf_expiry` defaults to `false`. When enabled, a recognized CDF
history expiry requests a full rescore of the pinned source snapshot and model
selection through the separate `recover_predictions` task inside this score job.
This option is independent of `model_change_mode` and supports single models,
competitions and model sets. It does not train models or change registry aliases.
The task can read and score the complete source, so the existing input limits
still apply. It replaces the compatible prediction output atomically after
checking source/output identities and the expected output version. Table IDs,
grants and named views remain intact. An exact committed recovery retry is a
no-op; ordinary incremental scoring then resumes from its saved watermark.

Permission/connectivity failures, disabled CDF, source replacement, schema errors
and readable source UPDATE/DELETE changes retain their existing failure behavior.
Recovery is not a general retry or authorization to accept those changes. Older
model-version generations remain available; rollback within the rebuilt generation
depends on Delta history retention. Open `scoring_report` for the completed result
on either the ordinary scoring or recovery branch.

If scoring fails after promotion, retry score independently. Inspect saved
MLflow evidence and registry state before a fresh lifecycle run; lifecycle repair
and retry are rejected because effects may already have committed.
Configure job/task time limits, optional score retries and email/webhook events in
`deployment/variables.yml`, or override them for a target. See
[operational settings and score-only recovery](deployment/README.md#time-limits-retries-and-notifications).
Limits default to unlimited, retries to zero and notification recipients to empty.
See [failure evidence and pinned replay][replay] and [recovery][schedules].

Keep `workflow_contract: "3"` fixed in the generated notebook parameters.
`config_version: 1` is the separate workflow JSON schema version. Changing
`score_handoff` requires updating `deployed_score_handoff` wherever present in
`resources/train.job.yml` and `resources/score.job.yml`, then redeploying the
matching graph/notebooks/wheel.

Changing `auto_rebuild_on_cdf_expiry` also requires regenerating and redeploying
the matching score graph, notebooks and wheel. Its generated
`deployed_auto_rebuild_on_cdf_expiry` parameter must match the JSON boolean in
both job definitions. Editing only `config/inference.yml` is rejected. Leave
the flag disabled for existing projects until the recovery tasks are deployed.

